

                            Star Battles

                          by Chris Nystrom

                       Beta Test Version 0.10

                             (c) 1990




              Features -

                 - Multi-Ship Space Fleet Conflict
                 - Multiple Players
                 - 22 Different Ship Types
                 - 13 Scenarios Included
                 - Capability to Design your own Scenarios
                 - Easy to Use Interface
                 - Supports Mouse


   This program should run on any compatible with at least 256K memory, running
DOS 2.0 or better. A hard drive or a second disk drive would be conveinent,
along with a mouse. A graphics monitor is not required, but a color monitor
would enhance the look of this program. Joysticks are not supported.
   The first thing to do is to make a backup copy of both disks. STAR BATTLES
is not copy protected. One way to do this might be to place STAR BATTLES in
drive A:, a blank formatted disk in drive B:, and type:

      A:COPY *.* B:

   If you are using a hard drive, make a subdirectory, and copy all the files
into that subdirectory. Here is an example of how that might be done:

      C:
      MD STARBAT
      CD STARBAT
      COPY A:*.*

    To make this program run faster, set up a ramdisk to be used for temporary
files, using the supplied ramdisk utility, or your own ramdisk software. Add
the line:

       DEVICE=RAMDISK.SYS

to your CONFIG.SYS file, using EDLIN or another editor. If this does not sound
familiar to you consult your DOS reference, or just skip this section. Next,
add the line:

       RAMDISK 64

into your AUTOEXEC.BAT file, or just type it in from the DOS command line. 
A virtual disk drive of 64K will be set in RAM. It will use the next available
disk drive letter. Now set up a path to use for temporary files by adding the
line:

       SET TMP=C:\

into your AUTOEXEC.BAT file, or type in from the DOS command line. Use the
drive letter corresponding to your RAM disk.
    If you decide not to use a RAM disk, using the above command to direct
temporary files to your hard disk will speed up the game, since a hard drive
is much faster than a floppy drive.

    To run the game on a single drive system, put disk #1 in the drive and type
STARBAT. When it is time to to load in a scenario, put disk #2 in the drive.
    For 2 floppy drive systems, put disk #1 in drive A:, and disk #2 in
drive B:. Make sure the default drive is set for A:, and type STARBAT. When it
comes time to load in a scenario, and you are asked to input the path,
type B:\.
    If you have a hard drive then change your default directory to the one that
has the Star Battles programs and data files, and type STARBAT.


                        STAR BATTLES Instructions

   Star Battles is a tactical wargame simulating conflict between space
fleets. It is a strategy game, and not an arcade game. A player makes decisions
concerning the ships he controls, and then the computer processes the
turn. This continues until only one side is left, or the player is finished.
The simulation supports multiple players, whether on the same side, or
opposing sides.
   To begin, type STARBAT (to skip the opening, just type SB). A menu with the
choices below will appear:

      END TURN
      SAVE GAME
      LOAD GAME
      QUIT

   Select a choice use the cursor control (arrow) keys to go up or down. When
the selection desired is highlighted, push the ENTER key. If you are using a
mouse, roll it up or down to highlight your selection, then push the left
button.
   To start the game select LOAD GAME. Enter the path where the scenario data
files can be found, and then the name of the scenario (The descriptions of
each scenario are listed later). To learn how to play STAR BATTLES just
type 1. The status message 'Loading 1.DAT...' will appear.
   After the scenario is loaded, input the number of players. Enter 1. Next,
player 1's name will be requested. Enter Captain Kirk, or whatever name
desired. Next, assign a player to each fleet listed. You will use a menu that
includes the computer, and each player name. Assign the fleet to a player, or
to the computer to control.

   Next will be the player selection menu. The player names as well as SAVE,
LOAD, and QUIT will appear. The player selects his or her name to have access
to his or her fleets, or ships.
   SAVE is used to save a scenario to play later.
   LOAD is used to load a new scenario.
   QUIT can be selected to QUIT the game.
  
   Next will be a list of available fleets, or ships. Select the fleet or
ship desired. After the ship is selected, a list of options will appear:

   CARGO - This option will list cargo onboard. If docked use this option to
      transfer cargo. Computer controlled ships will automatically
      transfer cargo, if it is available.

   DAMAGE CONTROL - Provides a list of ship's equipment, and current repair
      status.

   LIBRARY COMPUTER - This option has 3 sub options. There is a Direction
      and Distance Calculator, a Location Calculator, and a Photon Torpedo
      Calculator.
         The Direction and Distance Calculator computes a direction and
      distance given any two points.
         The Location Calculator computes a location given a point, a
      direction, and a distance.
         The Torpedo Calculator gives recommended torpedo courses based upon
      targets identified by the ship's Directors. More on directors in the
      Sensors section.

   MAIN CONTROL - This option is used to control engineering equipment.

      Generators convert fuel to energy.
      Impulse Engines convert energy to velocity.
      Thrusters are used to provide directional control.
      The Switchboard is used to turn Sensors on, or off.

   MESSAGES - Select this option to read messages from the crew that came up
      while the last move was processed.

   NAVIGATION - There are 2 options here. Manuever or Dock.

         The Manuever Option will present the Scanner display, and request a
      heading, and an ordered velocity.
    
                                    000
                                    360
                                     :
                               270..<+>..090
                                     :
                                    180

                                 Headings

         Next, Enter a velocity from 0 to the maximum velocity for that class
      of ship. At least 1 Thruster online is required to be able to change
      your heading. If 2 thrusters are online, your heading will change faster.
      At least 1 Engine online is required to move, and the more engines
      online the faster the movement. Collision with stars or other ships could
      cause damage.

         A ships can only DOCK with one other ships at a time. In addition, the
      following conditions must be met:

            1. There must be no more than 1 unit of velocity difference
               between ships.
            2. The docking ship's heading must be within 10 degrees of the
               heading of other ship, unless the other ship is a Space Station.
            3. The other ship must have shields lowered.

   SENSORS - There are several different types of Sensors.

      SCANNER is a visual display of the surroundings. In addition, if the
         cursor is placed on a particular ship to scan, a status report on
         that ship will be presented, including an energy reading, shield
         status report, and damage percent report.
        
      SHORT RANGE SENSOR provides a coded display of your quadrant as well as
         2 quadrants around you in all directions. The 100's digit in the code
         is the number of smaller ships. Then 10's digit is the number of
         larger ships (such as space stations), and the 1's digit is the
         number of stars in that sector.
            For example: '213' indicates 2 smaller ships, 1 space station, and
         3 stars in that sector.

      LONG RANGE SENSOR is almost exactly like the short range sensor except
         that it displays 3 quadrants around you in all directions. Be aware
         that your enemies can locate you from very far away when you use your
         active sensors. In fact a Long Range Sensor is almost like a beacon.

      DIRECTORS are like scanners except that they are used to identify
         targets for phasers. You must have a director locked on to a target
         for your phaser to hit it.

      ESM stands for Energy Surviellence Measures, and it is used to pick up
         the bearing from which sensors are radiating from. This passive
         detection of other vessals does not inform others of your location.
         You can pick up other vessals via ESM much farther out than you can
         with active sensors such as Long Range Sensors, however you can not
         get distance information via ESM.
            Your enemy can defeat ESM by simply turning off his sensor.

   SHIELDS - Transfer energy to your shields to provide protection. This
      energy is lost every turn, but is automatically realocated each turn if
      it is available. Entering 0 units will turn off your shields.
         If you have only one shield it provides 360 degree protection.
         If you have two shields, shield #1 provides coverage for the foreward
      part of the ship, from 271 degrees to 090 degrees relative to the bow of
      the ship.
          If you have three shields then shield #1 provides coverage for the
      foreward part of the ship, from 301 degrees to 060 degrees. Shield #2
      provides coverage for starboard quarter from 061 degrees to 180 degrees.
      Shield #3 provides coverage for the port quarter from 181 degrees to 300
      degrees.
          If you have four shields then shield #1 provides coverage for the
      foreward part of the ship from 316 degrees to 045 degrees. Shield #2
      provides coverage for the starboard side of the ship from 046 degrees to
      135 degrees. Shield #3 provides coverage for the stern of the ship from
      136 degrees to 225 degrees. Shield #4 provides cover for the port side
      of the ship from 226 degrees to 315 degrees.

   WEAPONS - There are 2 types of weapons. Phasers and Photon Torpedoes.

      PHASERS are energy weapons. They are less powerful than torpedoes, but
         are more likely to hit the target. You must have the corresponding
         Director locked on target, or your Phaser will just shoot out into
         space. Directors are found in the SENSORS section.

      TORPEDOES are less likely to hit your opponent, but always cause serious
         damage when they do. You can get a torpedo course recommendation from
         the ships computer, but this information is only valid if your
         opponent maintains course and speed (which he or she probably won't).
         You can offset this by firing a spread of torpedoes if you have
         multiple tubes.


