                             FORMATION STEAMING

   As any good Commodore from the age of sail to the age of the impulse
engine knows, nothing can strike fear in an opponent more than a tight for-
mation of ships. It tells your opponent that you know what you are doing. You
are a competent and confident commander. It is the clearest most visible dis-
play of the skill of the officer in charge. Precise control of you ships also
serves the added purpose of preventing the torpedoing your own ships! Con-
trolling the disposition of your ships is not as easy as it looks and often
in battles formations can not be maintained, but the pride and satisfaction
derived from a tightly run formation is tremendous. I will only briefly dis-
cuss some of the basic formations and manuevers here.
   First a few terms. The Guide is the ship that all the other ships form up
on. True Bearing is bearing with respect to 000 (straight up). Relative
bearing is with respect to the bow of the ship. For example a ship is on
course 090 (right). The port beam is always 270 relative (to the bow), but
in this case is also 000 true.
   The simplest formation is a Column. The ships are all in a line in the
same direction as their course with the guide in the lead.
   A variation of a column is a Line Abreast. In this formation ships are
formed up on the beam of the guide.
   A Line of Bearing is a formation in which the ships are niether
ahead, behind, or abeam of the guide ship, but still in a line. For example,
if the formation course was 000 and the ships were all in a line on a bearing
135.
   A Diamond Formation is a formation where the 2nd and 3rd ships are at a
45 degree angle from the guide ship and the fourth ship is at a 45 deg angle
from the 2nd and 3rd ships, directly behind the guide, in a diamond shape.
   A Turn is a movement where all ships turn at the same time. Upon comple-
tion of the manuever each ship retains its original true bearing from the
guide, but its relative bearing has changed.
   A Corpen (also called wheeling) is a manuever where formation course is
altered without altering the arrangement of the ships. On completion of the
manuever each ship retains its original relative bearing from the guide, but
the true bearing has changed. Limitations on the amout of a wheel turn are:

         Column................180 deg.
         Line Abreast...........90 deg.
         Diamond................30 deg.

   When wheeling a column, the lead ship turns to the new course, and re-
maining ships follow behind the lead ship maintaining the column. When a line
abreast is wheeeled the end ship in the direction of the turn (pivot ship)
comes to the new course. Remaining ships increase speed and manuever inde-
pendantly to regain their original relative bearing from the guide.
   A Search Turn is used for altering course of a Line Abreast or Line of
Bearing formation when covering an area. The turn must be at least 45 deg,
but not more than 135 deg. The wing ship away from the direction of the turn
comes to the new course and becomes the guide. When this ship passes astern
of the next ship then this ship turns, and so on down the line until all
ships are on the new course.
   Another formation is a Screen. For example a convoy of merchant ships
could be grouped in the same sector, while escort ships could be placed in
adjacent sectors in the direction of the threat(s) to intercept any attacks
before they reach the defenceless merchant vessals.


                                 SCENARIO 1

   Load: 1

   Stardate: 2700

   Fleet Number 1: FSS Hotspur (1 Frigate)
   Fleet Number 2: KB IX-A (1 Barge)

      This is a basic scenario to give you practice with Star Battles. You are
given a mission of destroying a Klargon barge that was abandoned by the Klargon
Empire when it was expelled from these quadrants by the Federation fleet during
Galactic War I. Never fear! Barges have no weapons.
      Recommended configuration is for the computer to control fleet #2.


                                 SCENARIO 2

   Load: 2

   Stardate: 2891

   Fleet Number 1: FSS John Paul Jones (1 Destroyer)
   Fleet Number 2: KS Kasturi (1 Frigate)

      On stardate 2891 the Federation destroyer FSS John Paul Jones chanced
upon a solitary Klargon frigate. Although an uneven match, the Federation
Destroyer displayed the fighting qualities of her namesake (an 18th century
admiral from the planet Earth), destroying her advisary in a viscous fight.
Your mission is to do the same.
      Recommended configuration is for the computer to control fleet #2.


                                 SCENARIO 3

   Load: 3

   Stardate: 2952

   Fleet Number 1: FSS Lydia (1 Destroyer)
   Fleet Number 2: KS Kabul (1 Destroyer)

      An even match up was the duel between the Federation destroyer FSS Lydia
and the Klargon Destroyer KS Kabul. Although a long and grueling battle, both
ships survived the encounter, limping home with extensive damages. See if you
can do better.
      A single player can play either side. This would also be a good two
player scenario.


                                 SCENARIO 4

   Load: 4

   Stardate: 2983

   Fleet Number 1: FSS Prince Eugen (1 Heavy Cruiser)
   Fleet Number 2: KS Kagoshim (1 Heavy Cruiser)

      On stardate 2983 a Federation Heavy Cruiser FSS Prince Eugen met, fought
and eventually destroyed the Klargon Heavy Cruise KS Kagoshim, using superior
tactics.
      A single player can play either side, and this would also be a good two
player scenario.


                                 SCENARIO 5

   Load: 5

   Stardate: 2680

   Fleet Number 1: KS Kaluga (1 Heavy Cruiser)
   Fleet Number 2: Destroyer Squadron XXX (2 Destroyers)

   Two Federation destroyers FSS Mitscher and FSS Battleaxe confronted the
Klargon heavy cruiser KS Kaluga, but were unable to destroy her, before they
were too damaged to continue the engagement.
  

                                 SCENARIO 6

   Load: 6

   Stardate: 2886

   Fleet Number 1: FSS Enterprise (1 Heavy Cruiser)
   Fleet Number 2: Federation Merchant Fleet (1 Space Station, 1 Fueler,
                      3 Frieghters, 1 Transport, and 3 Tankers)
   Fleet Number 3: Klargon Invasion Force (3 Destroyers, 3 Frigates, 1 Torpedo
                      Craft, 2 Corvettes, 3 Patrollers)

      This scenario is equivalent to the scenario from the classic 'STAR TREK'
computer games. You are captain of the Federation Heavy Cruiser FSS Enterprise.
Your mission is to destroy the invading Klargon fleet. You start out near Star
Base FS #9 located at quadrant 10,10, sector 1,2.  In addition there is a
Federation Fueler FSS Brezina located aprroxiamately 5 quadrants away on a
bearing of 135. Your mission is urgent in that the Klargons are systematically
destroying the numerous Federation merchant shipping in the area. An attack on
the space station can only be expected. You will find that although a Heavy
Cruiser is a powerful ship, it is inadequate for covering a large area single
handedly. Shortly afterwards the Federation went to an age old tactic known as
the 'convoy system' where merchant shipping was grouped together to facilitate
the common defence. In addition a building program of numerous smaller vessals
was began to counter the Klargon menace.
      It is recommended that the computer control the Federation Merchant
Fleet, as well as the Klargon INvasion Force.


                                 SCENARIO 7

   Load: 7

   Stardate: 2976

   Fleet Number 1: KS Kashgar (1 BattleCruiser)
   Fleet Number 2: Cruiser Division Two (3 Light Cruisers)
  
      After careful deliberation the Federation decided to send 3 light
cruisers to attack and destroy the Klargon Battlecruiser KS Kashgar that was
menacing the quadrants of space near the Antaires IV reqion.


                                 Scenario 8

   Load: 8

   Stardate: 2750

   Fleet Number 1: FSS Agincourt (1 Dreadnought)
   Fleet Number 2: KS Kisuma (1 Dreadnought)

      In a rare occurance, on stardate 2750 a Federation dreadnought
FSS Agincourt met and fought with the Klargon dreadnought KS Kisuma. You can
recreate this famous battle that was an important turning point in Galactic
War I.


                                 Scenario 9

   Load: 9

   Stardate: 2531

   Fleet Number 1: FSS Yamato (1 Dreadnought)
   Fleet Number 2: Third Destruction Squadron (5 Destroyers)

      The Federation dreadnought FSS Yamato successfully fought off 5 Klargon
destroyers, using superior firepower, and by avoiding the hordes of torpedoes.


                                 Scenario 10

   Load: 10

   Stardate: 2672

   Fleet Number 1: FO Orion (1 Space Outpost)
   Fleet Number 2: KS Kediri (1 Heavy Cruiser)
   Fleet Number 3: Defence Force (3 Destroyers, 1 Frigate, 2 Torpedo Craft, 2
                      Corvettes, 3 Patrollers)
   Fleet Number 4: Support Force (1 Frigate, 1 Fueler, 1 Repair Ship)
   Fleet Number 5: Merchant Fleet (4 Frieghters, 1 Transport, 2 Tankers)

      On stardate 2672 the Klargon Heavy Cruiser Kediri penetrated deep into
Federation territory and began destroying Federation merchant shipping. The
Federation was caught off guard and had no heavy units in the area. Admiral
Jameson assembled a hodge podge force of small combatants and destroyed the
invader, earning for himself the Federation Legion of Honor.
      It is recommended that the computer be assigned Fleets 2, and 5.
Optionally a player may want to let the computer control fleets 1, and 4.


                                 Scenario 11

   Load: 11

   Stardate: 2533

   Fleet Number 1: FS #12 (1 Space Station)
   Fleet Number 2: Invicible Strike Force (1 Dreadnought, 1 Heavy Cruiser)
   Fleet Number 3: Battleship Division Six (1 Battleship, 1 BattleCruiser)
   Fleet Number 4: Convoy Escort Force (1 Light Cruiser, 1 Destroyer,
                      2 Frigates)
   Fleet Number 5: Merchant Fleet Convoy (1 Frieghter, 1 Transport, and
                      4 Tankers)

      The Federation was seeking to resupply Starbase #12 when it was learned
from Star Fleet Intelligence that the Klargons were going to attempt to destroy
the supply convoy which might nessitate abandoning the starbase. Star Fleet
Admiralty decided to send in a Battleship division to beef up the defence in
the area. Wisely the Klargons decided to avoid combat with the battleship
division and destroy the convoy. Results were mixed. Several merchant vessals
were destroyed, but enough got through that the starbase stayed in operation.


                                 Scenario 12

   Load: 12

   Stardate: 2844

   Fleet Number 1: Main Battle Fleet (3 Dreadnoughts, 4 Battleships,
                      2 BattleCruisers, 2 Frigates)
   Fleet Number 2: Steller Imperium (3 Dreadnoughts, 2 Battleships,
                      4 BattleCruisers, 2 Destroyers)

      In the engagement that broke the back of the Klargon empire, the
Federation Main Battle Fleet sortied to destroy the Klargon main fleet, the
vaunted Steller Imperium. Fleet Admiral Nimu became a hero of the Federation
by expertly divided the enemy column, and concentrating his forces, almost
completely annhilating the Klargon fleet.


                                 Scenario 13

   Load: 13

   Stardate: 2553

   Fleet Number 1: Second Fleet (2 Dreadnoughts, 1 Battleship,
                      3 Battlecruisers, 1 Heavy Cruiser, 1 Destroyer,
                      1 Frigate, 1 Space Station)
   Fleet Number 2: Galactic Imperium (2 Battleships, 2 Battlecruisers, 1 Light
                      Cruiser, 1 Destroyer)
   Fleet Number 3: Fleet Z-3 (2 Battleships, 2 BattleCruisers, 1 Light Cruiser,
                      1 Frigate)

      In an attempt to widen the war effort and to compensate for the loss of
the Steller Imperium, the Klargons entered into a treaty with the Zornians, to
jointly attack the Federation. This attack was designed to destroy Space
Station #18 which was the homeport of the Federation Second Fleet. Admiral
Fleer was able to defeat this threat by attacking each fleet seperately.

